diff --git a/tasks/muse_attention_sliding/description.md b/tasks/muse_attention_sliding/description.md
index 46a2a00..c9f855b 100644
--- a/tasks/muse_attention_sliding/description.md
+++ b/tasks/muse_attention_sliding/description.md
@@ -12,3 +12,5 @@ The task uses page size 1, 16 query heads, 2 KV heads, head width 64, window 204
 For request b and new token j, normalize Q and K separately per head with `rsqrt(mean(x*x)+1e-5)` in FP32, then apply interleaved RoPE at position `seq_lens[b]+j`. Quantize new K and V to FP8 E4M3 after dividing by their respective scales, and write them at `cache_loc`. Attention is causal and includes the current token and up to 2047 preceding tokens. For each Q head use its shared KV head, logits `dot(Q,K)*5.4730064863875/8`, softmax over visible keys, and FP8 cache values multiplied by their scale. RMS normalize each 64-element attention output with epsilon `1e-5`, multiply by sigmoid(gate), and round to BF16. Match the reference's FP8 query behavior within the challenge tolerance.
 
 Return `(out, k_cache, v_cache)`, where `out` is BF16 `[batch,length,1024]` and the caches are the mutated inputs. Cache writes are part of the contract. Valid groups must have `0 <= seq_lens[b]`, `seq_lens[b]+length <= table_len`, distinct in-bounds physical pages for all used logical positions and `cache_loc[b*length+j] == page_table[b,seq_lens[b]+j]`. Use fresh data and no caching of input-derived results. Generate normal and outlier values with positive finite scales; avoid NaNs and infinities. The challenge validates both outputs and cache writes with its own relative-L2 tolerances, page-64 and scale edge cases. This task screens only page-1 sliding shapes; any candidate needs full official validation.
+
+For this H200 task, CUDA candidates must begin with `// fk-cuda-cflags: -gencode=arch=compute_90a,code=sm_90a` so the generated extension targets the GPU. Launch on PyTorch’s current CUDA stream, check CUDA launch errors, and validate numerical outputs and cache mutations before timing.
